  "hints": [
    "The best rectangle uses some building's full height. For building i, how far can that rectangle stretch left and right?",
    "It stretches until the first strictly shorter building on each side. A monotonic stack finds those boundaries.",
    "Keep indices with increasing heights. When a lower (or equal) height arrives at i, pop index t: its right limit is i and its left limit is the new stack top + 1. Add a sentinel height 0 at the end to flush the stack."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function largestRectangle(heights) {\n  const stack = []\n  let best = 0\n  for (let i = 0; i <= heights.length; i++) {\n    const h = i < heights.length ? heights[i] : 0\n    while (stack.length && heights[stack[stack.length - 1]] >= h) {\n      const height = heights[stack.pop()]\n      const left = stack.length ? stack[stack.length - 1] + 1 : 0\n      best = Math.max(best, height * (i - left))\n    }\n    stack.push(i)\n  }\n  return best\n}\n",
    "py": "def largest_rectangle(heights):\n    stack = []\n    best = 0\n    for i in range(len(heights) + 1):\n        h = heights[i] if i < len(heights) else 0\n        while stack and heights[stack[-1]] >= h:\n            height = heights[stack.pop()]\n            left = stack[-1] + 1 if stack else 0\n            best = max(best, height * (i - left))\n        stack.append(i)\n    return best\n",
    "explanation": "For each popped bar, the bars on the stack below it are shorter and the bar that caused the pop is not taller, so the bar's maximal rectangle spans exactly between them. Every rectangle in the optimum is maximal for its shortest bar, so checking each bar once finds the answer."
